  <!-- ===================== MECHANICAL ADVANTAGE (NEW!) ===================== -->

  <div class="section active" id="ma">
    <div class="section-title">Mechanical Advantage (MA)</div>
    <div class="section-sub">The BIG concept — how much a machine multiplies your force. A larger number = machine does MORE work for you.</div>

```
<div class="info-block yellow">
  <div class="tip-label">🧠 What Is Mechanical Advantage?</div>
  Mechanical Advantage (MA) tells you <strong>how many times a machine multiplies the force you put in.</strong><br><br>
  • MA = <strong>2</strong> → the machine doubles your force (you push with 10 N, machine pushes with 20 N)<br>
  • MA = <strong>0.5</strong> → the machine cuts your force in half (but increases speed/distance)<br>
  • MA = <strong>1</strong> → no force advantage, just redirects force (like a single fixed pulley)<br><br>
  <strong>A LARGER MA number = the machine is doing MORE of the work for you!</strong>
</div>

<div class="divider"></div>
<h3>THE TWO MA FORMULAS — KNOW BOTH!</h3>

<div class="two-col">
  <div class="mini-card">
    <div class="mini-title">Formula 1 — Using FORCE</div>
    <div class="mini-formula">MA = Output Force<br>      ──────────<br>       Input Force</div>
    <div class="mini-note">Output force = what the machine produces (resistance force / weight of object)<br>Input force = what YOU apply (effort force)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="mini-card">
    <div class="mini-title">Formula 2 — Using DISTANCE (Levers)</div>
    <div class="mini-formula">MA = Input Arm<br>      ──────────<br>      Output Arm</div>
    <div class="mini-note">Input arm = distance from fulcrum to where YOU push<br>Output arm = distance from fulcrum to the load</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="warn-block">
  <strong>⚠️ Why two different formulas?</strong> Both measure the same thing (MA) but from two different angles. Formula 1 uses actual measured forces. Formula 2 uses distances (for levers). In a perfect world they'd give the same answer — but friction makes them slightly different. That's why we have IMA vs AMA.
</div>

<div class="divider"></div>
<h3>EXAMPLES — FORMULA 1 (Force)</h3>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Example 1 — Simple</div>
  <div class="ex-q">You push with <strong>15 N</strong> (input/effort force). The machine lifts an object weighing <strong>60 N</strong> (output/resistance force). What is the MA?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = Output Force ÷ Input Force</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = 60 ÷ 15</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">MA = 4 &nbsp;← machine multiplies your force 4 times!</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label purple">Example 2 — Inclined Plane (from your worksheet!)</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Without a ramp, it takes <strong>120 g</strong> to lift an object directly. With the ramp, you only need <strong>75 g</strong> of input force. What is the MA?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = Output Force ÷ Input Force</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = 120 ÷ 75</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">MA = 1.6</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label green">Example 3 — Ramp B from your worksheet!</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Output force (without ramp) = <strong>120 g</strong>. Input force (with ramp) = <strong>20 g</strong>. Find MA.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = 120 ÷ 20</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">MA = 6 &nbsp;← ramp multiplies your force 6 times!</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="divider"></div>
<h3>EXAMPLES — FORMULA 2 (Distance / Lever Arm)</h3>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label pink">Lever Example 1</div>
  <div class="ex-q">You push a lever. Your hand is <strong>40 cm</strong> from the fulcrum (input arm). The rock is <strong>10 cm</strong> from the fulcrum (output arm). What is the MA?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = Input Arm ÷ Output Arm</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = 40 ÷ 10</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">MA = 4 &nbsp;← lever multiplies your force 4 times!</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label pink">Lever Example 2</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Input arm = <strong>37 m</strong>. Output arm (resistance distance) = <strong>12 m</strong>. Find MA.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = Input Arm ÷ Output Arm</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = 37 ÷ 12</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">MA = 3.08</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label pink">Lever Example 3 — MA less than 1</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Input arm = <strong>24 m</strong>. Output arm = <strong>30 m</strong>. Find MA. What does this mean?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = 24 ÷ 30</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">MA = 0.8 &nbsp;← less than 1! No force advantage.</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>This lever trades force for speed/distance (Class 3 lever, like your arm)</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="divider"></div>
<h3>REARRANGEMENTS — Solve for what you don't have</h3>

<div class="formula-card purple">
  <div class="formula-label">All 3 Versions — Force Formula</div>
  <div class="formula">MA = Output Force ÷ Input Force</div>
  <div class="rearrange">
    <div class="rearrange-label">🔄 Solve for Output Force</div>
    <div class="formula" style="font-size:15px;">Output Force = MA × Input Force</div>
  </div>
  <div class="rearrange">
    <div class="rearrange-label">🔄 Solve for Input Force</div>
    <div class="formula" style="font-size:15px;">Input Force = Output Force ÷ MA</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label purple">Find Input Force</div>
  <div class="ex-q">A machine has MA = <strong>5</strong>. You need to lift a <strong>300 N</strong> object. How much force do YOU need to apply?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>Input Force = Output Force ÷ MA</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>= 300 ÷ 5</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 60 N &nbsp;← you only need 60 N to lift 300 N!</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label purple">Find Output Force</div>
  <div class="ex-q">MA = <strong>8</strong>. You apply <strong>45 N</strong>. What force does the machine produce?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>Output Force = MA × Input Force</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>= 8 × 45</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 360 N</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="divider"></div>
<h3>IMA vs AMA — THE DIFFERENCE</h3>

<div class="info-block purple">
  <div class="tip-label">Theoretical vs Actual</div>
  <strong>IMA (Ideal MA)</strong> = calculated from distances/measurements. Assumes NO friction. This is the <strong>theoretical</strong> (perfect world) value.<br><br>
  <strong>AMA (Actual MA)</strong> = calculated from real measured forces. Includes friction losses. This is the <strong>actual</strong> (real world) value.<br><br>
  AMA is <strong>always less than or equal to</strong> IMA because friction eats some of your effort.
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label purple">IMA vs AMA Side-by-Side (Ramp A from your worksheet)</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Ramp A: Length = <strong>38 cm</strong>, Height = <strong>3 cm</strong>. Without ramp = <strong>120 g</strong>. With ramp = <strong>15 g</strong>.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">IMA:</span><span>= Length ÷ Height = 38 ÷ 3 = <span class="step-ans">12.67</span></span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">AMA:</span><span>= Output ÷ Input = 120 ÷ 15 = <span class="step-ans">8</span></span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">Eff:</span><span>= (AMA ÷ IMA) × 100 = (8 ÷ 12.67) × 100 = <span class="step-ans">63.1%</span></span></div>
  </div>
</div>
```

