  <!-- ===================== EFFICIENCY ===================== -->

  <div class="section" id="efficiency">
    <div class="section-title">Efficiency</div>
    <div class="section-sub">How much of your work input actually becomes useful work output?</div>

```
<div class="info-block">
  <div class="tip-label">What is it?</div>
  Efficiency tells you how "good" a machine is at not wasting energy. A perfect machine = <strong>100%</strong>. Real machines are always less than 100% because friction wastes some energy as heat.
</div>

<div class="formula-card">
  <div class="formula-label">Main Formula — Work Method</div>
  <div class="formula">Efficiency = (Work Output ÷ Work Input) × 100%</div>
  <div class="formula-note">Work is measured in <strong>Joules (J)</strong>. Answer is always a <strong>percent %</strong>.</div>
  <div class="rearrange">
    <div class="rearrange-label">🔄 Rearrangements</div>
    <div class="formula" style="font-size:15px;">Work Output = (Efficiency% ÷ 100) × Work Input</div>
    <div class="formula" style="font-size:15px;">Work Input = Work Output ÷ (Efficiency% ÷ 100)</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="formula-card">
  <div class="formula-label">Main Formula — MA Method</div>
  <div class="formula">Efficiency = (AMA ÷ IMA) × 100%</div>
  <div class="formula-note">Use this when you have both IMA and AMA already calculated.</div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Example 1 — Find Efficiency</div>
  <div class="ex-q">A cell phone charger uses <strong>4.83 J</strong> but only <strong>1.31 J</strong> goes into the battery. How efficient?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>Efficiency = (1.31 ÷ 4.83) × 100</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 27.1%</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Example 2 — Find Work Output</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Bicycle is <strong>92% efficient</strong>. Cyclist inputs <strong>100 J</strong>. How many joules move the bike?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>Work Out = (92 ÷ 100) × 100 = 0.92 × 100</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 92 J</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Example 3 — Find Work Input</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Engine is <strong>15% efficient</strong>. Needs <strong>15,000 J</strong> of output. How much input?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>Work In = 15,000 ÷ (15 ÷ 100) = 15,000 ÷ 0.15</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 100,000 J</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label green">Example 4 — Efficiency using AMA/IMA</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Ramp: IMA = <strong>6</strong>, AMA = <strong>5.22</strong>. What is the efficiency?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>Efficiency = (AMA ÷ IMA) × 100</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>= (5.22 ÷ 6) × 100</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 87%</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="warn-block">
  <strong>⚠️ Common Mistake:</strong> 60% as a decimal = 0.60. Always divide by 100 to convert percent to decimal before multiplying!
</div>
```

  </div>

  <!-- ===================== IMA & AMA ===================== -->

  <div class="section" id="ima-ama">
    <div class="section-title">IMA &amp; AMA</div>
    <div class="section-sub">Ideal (theoretical) vs. Actual (real) Mechanical Advantage</div>

```
<div class="info-block purple">
  <div class="tip-label">The Big Picture</div>
  <strong>IMA</strong> = uses distances/measurements. No friction assumed. Theoretical.<br>
  <strong>AMA</strong> = uses real measured forces. Includes friction. Actual.<br>
  <strong>AMA is ALWAYS ≤ IMA</strong> in the real world.<br>
  <strong>Efficiency</strong> = (AMA ÷ IMA) × 100%
</div>

<div class="formula-card">
  <div class="formula-label">IMA — Inclined Plane (Ramp)</div>
  <div class="formula">IMA = Length of Ramp ÷ Height of Ramp</div>
  <div class="formula-note">Also: effort distance ÷ resistance distance</div>
</div>

<div class="formula-card">
  <div class="formula-label">AMA — Any Machine</div>
  <div class="formula">AMA = Resistance Force ÷ Effort Force</div>
  <div class="formula-note"><strong>Resistance force</strong> = weight of object (what you fight against)<br><strong>Effort force</strong> = how hard YOU push/pull</div>
  <div class="rearrange">
    <div class="rearrange-label">🔄 Rearrangements</div>
    <div class="formula" style="font-size:14px;">Resistance Force = AMA × Effort Force</div>
    <div class="formula" style="font-size:14px;">Effort Force = Resistance Force ÷ AMA</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label purple">Full 3-Step Example</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Ramp: <strong>12 m</strong> long, <strong>2 m</strong> tall. You push with <strong>150 N</strong>. Box weighs <strong>783 N</strong>. Find IMA, AMA, and Efficiency.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">1.</span><span>IMA = 12 ÷ 2 = <span class="step-ans">6</span></span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">2.</span><span>AMA = 783 ÷ 150 = <span class="step-ans">5.22</span></span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">3.</span><span>Efficiency = (5.22 ÷ 6) × 100 = <span class="step-ans">87%</span></span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label purple">Ramp D from your worksheet!</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Ramp D: Length = <strong>31 cm</strong>, Height = <strong>14 cm</strong>. Without ramp = <strong>120 g</strong>. With ramp = <strong>60 g</strong>.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">IMA</span><span>= 31 ÷ 14 = <span class="step-ans">2.21</span></span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">AMA</span><span>= 120 ÷ 60 = <span class="step-ans">2</span></span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">Eff</span><span>= (2 ÷ 2.21) × 100 = <span class="step-ans">90.5%</span></span></div>
  </div>
</div>
```

  </div>

  <!-- ===================== INCLINED PLANE ===================== -->

  <div class="section" id="inclined">
    <div class="section-title">Inclined Plane (Ramp)</div>
    <div class="section-sub">A flat surface tilted at an angle — the longer/shallower the ramp, the easier the push</div>

```
<div class="info-block yellow">
  <div class="tip-label">Key Idea</div>
  A ramp lets you use <strong>less force</strong> over a <strong>longer distance</strong> to do the same work. The gentler (shallower) the ramp = <strong>higher MA</strong> = easier. The steeper the ramp = <strong>lower MA</strong> = harder.
</div>

<div class="formula-card">
  <div class="formula-label">MA of Inclined Plane</div>
  <div class="formula">MA = Slope Length ÷ Height</div>
  <div class="formula-note">Slope length = the long side of the ramp you travel along<br>Height = the vertical rise (how tall it is)</div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Ramp A — From Your Lab</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Length = <strong>38 cm</strong>, Height = <strong>3 cm</strong>. MA from dimensions?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = 38 ÷ 3</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 12.67 (very gentle ramp = big advantage)</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Ramp B — From Your Lab</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Length = <strong>30.5 cm</strong>, Height = <strong>7 cm</strong>.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = 30.5 ÷ 7</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 4.36</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Ramp C — From Your Lab</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Length = <strong>34 cm</strong>, Height = <strong>10 cm</strong>.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = 34 ÷ 10</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 3.4</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Ramp D — From Your Lab</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Length = <strong>31 cm</strong>, Height = <strong>14 cm</strong>.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>MA = 31 ÷ 14</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 2.21 (steep ramp = lowest advantage)</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="info-block">
  <div class="tip-label">Conclusions from the Lab</div>
  <strong>Ramp A had the GREATEST mechanical advantage (12.67)</strong> because it had the longest slope and shortest height — the most gradual incline.<br><br>
  The two methods of measuring MA (from dimensions vs from forces) give <strong>different values</strong> because real-world friction reduces the actual MA below the ideal. The difference was large for steep ramps and smaller for shallow ones.
</div>

<div class="info-block yellow">
  <div class="tip-label">Conclusions Question 2 — Engineer Ramp</div>
  If you need to move a wheelbarrow up 100 feet, use the ramp with the <strong>highest MA (most gradual slope, like Ramp A)</strong> because you need to apply the least force — important for a heavy wheelbarrow over a long distance.
</div>

<div class="warn-block">
  <strong>⚠️ Sources of Error in the Lab:</strong> Friction on the surface, not measuring the spring scale while it's moving, measuring length/height inaccurately, the object slipping or tilting, and human error reading the scale.
</div>
```

  </div>

  <!-- ===================== GEARS ===================== -->

  <div class="section" id="gears">
    <div class="section-title">Gear Ratios</div>
    <div class="section-sub">How many times does one gear spin compared to another?</div>

```
<div class="info-block">
  <div class="tip-label">Key Idea</div>
  Gears are <strong>round objects with teeth</strong>. More teeth = spins slower. Fewer teeth = spins faster.<br>
  Gears touching each other always spin in <strong>opposite directions</strong>.
</div>

<div class="formula-card">
  <div class="formula-label">Gear Ratio / Output Turns</div>
  <div class="formula">Output Turns = (Input Teeth ÷ Output Teeth) × Input Turns</div>
  <div class="formula-note">Input gear = the gear you're turning (driver). Output gear = the gear being driven.</div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Example 1</div>
  <div class="ex-q"><strong>36-tooth</strong> gear turns <strong>3 times</strong> → connected to <strong>12-tooth</strong> gear. How many times does the small gear turn?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>Output = (36 ÷ 12) × 3 = 3 × 3</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 9 turns</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Example 2 — Big to Small</div>
  <div class="ex-q"><strong>72-tooth</strong> gear makes <strong>1 turn</strong> → connected to <strong>12-tooth</strong> gear. How many turns does the small gear make?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>Output = (72 ÷ 12) × 1</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 6 turns</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label green">Example 3 — Three Gears</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Top=12, Middle=24, Bottom=36 teeth. What's the total gear ratio?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">1.</span><span>Ratio 1 (Top→Middle) = 12 ÷ 24 = 0.5</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">2.</span><span>Ratio 2 (Middle→Bottom) = 24 ÷ 36 = 0.67</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">3.</span><span>Total = 0.5 × 0.67 = <span class="step-ans">0.34</span></span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="warn-block">
  <strong>⚠️ Direction:</strong> With 3 gears — Top goes RIGHT → Middle goes LEFT → Bottom goes RIGHT again (same as top).
</div>
```

  </div>

  <!-- ===================== LEVERS ===================== -->

  <div class="section" id="levers">
    <div class="section-title">Levers</div>
    <div class="section-sub">A bar that pivots on a fulcrum — know your 3 classes AND both MA formulas</div>

```
<div class="info-block yellow">
  <div class="tip-label">3 Classes — Quick Recall</div>
  <strong>Class 1</strong> — Fulcrum in MIDDLE. Ex: seesaw, pliers, scissors.<br>
  <strong>Class 2</strong> — Load (output) in MIDDLE. Ex: wheelbarrow, bottle opener. Always MA &gt; 1.<br>
  <strong>Class 3</strong> — Effort (input) in MIDDLE. Ex: your arm/elbow, tennis racket, tweezers. MA &lt; 1 (multiplies speed, not force).
</div>

<div class="two-col">
  <div class="mini-card">
    <div class="mini-title">IMA of Lever</div>
    <div class="mini-formula">IMA = Effort Distance<br>       ──────────────<br>       Resistance Distance<br>(from fulcrum)</div>
    <div class="mini-note">Use distances (lengths of arms). Theoretical value.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="mini-card">
    <div class="mini-title">AMA / MA of Lever</div>
    <div class="mini-formula">MA = Output Force<br>      ──────────<br>      Input Force<br>(actual forces)</div>
    <div class="mini-note">Use measured forces in Newtons. Actual value.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Full Lever Example</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Effort arm = <strong>5.8 m</strong>, Resistance arm = <strong>6.3 m</strong>. Effort force = <strong>57.7 N</strong>. Load = <strong>45 N</strong>. Find IMA, AMA, Efficiency.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">IMA</span><span>= 5.8 ÷ 6.3 = <span class="step-ans">0.92</span></span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">AMA</span><span>= 45 ÷ 57.7 = <span class="step-ans">0.78</span></span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">Eff</span><span>= (0.78 ÷ 0.92) × 100 = <span class="step-ans">~84.8%</span></span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label pink">AMA Lever Practice</div>
  <div class="ex-q">You push down with <strong>75 N</strong> (effort). Object weighs <strong>597 N</strong> (resistance). What is the AMA?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>AMA = 597 ÷ 75</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 7.96</span></div>
  </div>
</div>
```

  </div>

  <!-- ===================== PULLEYS ===================== -->

  <div class="section" id="pulleys">
    <div class="section-title">Pulleys</div>
    <div class="section-sub">Count the supporting ropes — that's your IMA</div>

```
<div class="info-block">
  <div class="tip-label">THE Rule</div>
  <strong>IMA = number of ropes supporting the load.</strong> The rope you pull (going up over the top to YOUR hand) does NOT count.
</div>

<div class="formula-card">
  <div class="formula-label">Pulley Formulas</div>
  <div class="formula">IMA = # of supporting ropes</div>
  <div class="formula">AMA = Load (resistance force) ÷ Effort force</div>
  <div class="formula">Force per rope = Load ÷ IMA</div>
  <div class="formula">Efficiency = (AMA ÷ IMA) × 100%</div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Pulley Efficiency — Problem 12</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Pulley system with <strong>5 supporting ropes</strong>. Load = <strong>100 N</strong>. You pull with <strong>22 N</strong>.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">IMA</span><span>= 5 (count the ropes)</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">AMA</span><span>= 100 ÷ 22 = 4.55</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">Eff</span><span>= (4.55 ÷ 5) × 100 = <span class="step-ans">91%</span></span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Single Fixed Pulley</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Load = <strong>30 N</strong>. You pull with <strong>50 N</strong>. Efficiency?</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">IMA</span><span>= 1</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">AMA</span><span>= 30 ÷ 50 = 0.6</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">Eff</span><span>= (0.6 ÷ 1) × 100 = <span class="step-ans">60%</span></span></div>
  </div>
</div>
```

  </div>

  <!-- ===================== WHEEL & AXLE ===================== -->

  <div class="section" id="wheel">
    <div class="section-title">Wheel &amp; Axle</div>
    <div class="section-sub">Big circle (wheel) + small circle (axle) = special 360° lever</div>

```
<div class="info-block">
  <div class="tip-label">Key Idea</div>
  Turn the <strong>wheel</strong> → get more force at the axle (but slower). Turn the <strong>axle</strong> → get more speed at the wheel (but less force).<br>
  Big wheel + tiny axle = <strong>huge MA</strong>.
</div>

<div class="formula-card">
  <div class="formula-label">IMA of Wheel &amp; Axle</div>
  <div class="formula">IMA = Radius of Wheel ÷ Radius of Axle</div>
  <div class="formula" style="font-size:15px;">OR: Diameter of Wheel ÷ Diameter of Axle</div>
  <div class="formula-note">Use whichever you're given — just be consistent! Don't mix radius and diameter.</div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Example 1</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Wheel diameter = <strong>12 cm</strong>. Axle diameter = <strong>1.5 cm</strong>.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>IMA = 12 ÷ 1.5</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 8</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="example-card">
  <div class="ex-label">Example 2</div>
  <div class="ex-q">Wheel = <strong>13 cm</strong> diameter. Axle = <strong>3 cm</strong> diameter.</div>
  <div class="ex-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">→</span><span>IMA = 13 ÷ 3</span></div>
    <div class="step-line"><span class="step-num">✓</span><span class="step-ans">= 4.33</span></div>
  </div>
</div>
```

  </div>

  <!-- ===================== CHEAT SHEET ===================== -->

  <div class="section" id="cheatsheet">
    <div class="section-title">Master Cheat Sheet</div>
    <div class="section-sub">Every single formula — print this in your brain before the test</div>

```
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Concept</th><th>Formula</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>MA (Force)</strong></td><td class="mono">MA = Output Force ÷ Input Force</td><td>Output = resistance/weight. Input = effort/you.</td></tr>
