</table>

<div class="divider"></div>
<h3>KEY FACTS TO MEMORIZE</h3>

<div class="two-col">
  <div class="info-block yellow" style="margin-bottom:0">
    <div class="tip-label">MA Quick Rules</div>
    <strong>MA &gt; 1</strong> = machine multiplies force ✓<br>
    <strong>MA = 1</strong> = just redirects force<br>
    <strong>MA &lt; 1</strong> = multiplies speed/distance<br>
    <strong>Bigger MA = easier = less force needed</strong>
  </div>
  <div class="info-block purple" style="margin-bottom:0">
    <div class="tip-label">IMA vs AMA</div>
    <strong>IMA</strong> = distances = theoretical = perfect world<br>
    <strong>AMA</strong> = forces = actual = real world<br>
    <strong>AMA always ≤ IMA</strong> (friction!)<br>
    <strong>Efficiency = AMA÷IMA × 100%</strong>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="height:14px"></div>

<div class="info-block">
  <div class="tip-label">Lever Classes</div>
  <strong>Class 1</strong> — Fulcrum in middle (seesaw, pliers) — can be &gt;1 or &lt;1<br>
  <strong>Class 2</strong> — Load in middle (wheelbarrow) — always &gt;1 MA<br>
  <strong>Class 3</strong> — Effort in middle (your arm, tweezers) — always &lt;1 MA
</div>

<div class="warn-block">
  <strong>🎯 Universal Test Strategy — 3 Steps:</strong><br>
  <strong>Step 1:</strong> Find IMA (from distances, rope count, or dimensions)<br>
  <strong>Step 2:</strong> Find AMA (resistance force ÷ effort force)<br>
  <strong>Step 3:</strong> Efficiency = (AMA ÷ IMA) × 100%<br><br>
  If a problem only asks for one step — just do that step. If it gives you forces, use them for AMA. If it gives you distances, use them for IMA.
</div>
